Background. In delegated quantum computation, a computationally weak (typically classical or nearly classical) verifier wishes to delegate a quantum computation to an untrusted quantum prover in such a way that (i) blindness: the prover learns nothing about the computation beyond its size, and (ii) verifiability: the verifier can detect, except with negligible probability, any deviation of the prover from the prescribed protocol. Three landmark protocols achieving variants of these goals are:
  (a) the universal blind quantum computation protocol of Broadbent, Fitzsimons, and Kashefi, in which a verifier capable of preparing single-qubit states delegates a measurement-based quantum computation to a quantum server;
  (b) the interactive proofs for quantum computation of Aharonov, Ben-Or, Eban, and Mahadev, giving a verifier with limited quantum capabilities an interactive protocol whose soundness is information-theoretic;
  (c) Mahadev's classical verification of quantum computation, in which a fully classical polynomial-time verifier interacts with a BQP prover and soundness rests on the (post-quantum) hardness of Learning With Errors.

A protocol "relativizes" if, for every oracle O, the same protocol, with all parties (verifier and prover) granted query access to O, still satisfies its completeness, soundness, and (where applicable) blindness guarantees for the computation of arbitrary polynomial-time quantum circuits with O-gates.

Problem. Determine whether the three protocols above are inherently non-relativizing, in the sense that no protocol achieving the same security properties (blindness in case (a); information-theoretic verification of BQP computation by a nearly-classical verifier in case (b); classical verification of BQP under a standard cryptographic assumption in case (c)) can be made to work relative to an arbitrary oracle O when both the verifier and the prover are given query access to O. Equivalently: do there exist relativizing variants of these protocols, or can one prove an oracle O relative to which no such protocol exists?

